## Warehouse Simulation – Engineering Rules

### Purpose
- Goal: Compare stigmergy-like distributed shared memory (DSM) vs centralized memory for multi-robot task execution in a warehouse.
- Outcome: Quantify scalability, efficiency, and coordination overhead under varying load and topology.

### Coding
- Avoid inline comments. File header comments are just enough. 

### Modes and Feature Flags
- Simulation modes: distributed (default) vs centralized; inject strategy via a common interface.
- Time base: Fixed step or LF ticks; map step_interval (ms) to model.step_duration_s.

### Core Invariants
- Determinism: seed + config => reproducible runs.
- Task lifecycle: created → available → claimed → completed|failed|expired (timestamps recorded).
- Safety: Node capacity respected; head‑on collisions prevented via edge reservations.
- AoI: Reads include age; violations counted against aoi_threshold.

### Agent Behavior
- State machine: IDLE → NAVIGATING → WORKING → IDLE.
- Claiming: Prefer nearby tasks within search_radius; retry limited times; release unreachable tasks.
- Movement: Congestion-aware pathfinding; lateral escape; bounded backoff; use edge reservations.
- Utilization: utilization = working_steps / total_steps.

### Shared Memory Designs
- Distributed (stigmergy):
  - Layers: task_signal, flow_trace, jam_signal; timestamped writes; read_window returns window + peak.
  - Partitioning pluggable (spatial, spectral, …); halo_radius governs boundary exchange.
  - Metrics: reads, writes, gossip_messages, aoi_violations, coordination_time, partition_quality.
- Centralized:
  - Single authority with same API surface; model/agents unchanged.
  - Include coordinator latency/queueing in coordination_overhead.

### Metrics (collected in both modes)
- Performance: tasks_completed, tasks_failed, completion_rate, average_completion_time, agent_utilization, total_distance_traveled.
- Coordination: total_reads, total_writes, gossip_messages, aoi_violations, partition_quality, coordination_overhead.
- Time Series: step, cumulative completions, dsm_reads, dsm_writes.

### Configuration Conventions (experiments/configs.yaml)
- warehouse: size, storage_regions, sortation_regions.
- agents: count, initial_positions|"random", optional failure_schedule.
- dsm: partition_strategy, memory_owners, halo_radius, aoi_threshold.
- tasks: arrival_rate, types, priorities.
- simulation: duration, step_interval (ms), optional mode (distributed|centralized).
- seed: fix randomness across model and routing.

### Experiment Design
- Single-variable sweeps: change one factor per sweep (agents, arrival_rate, partition_strategy, aoi_threshold).
- Run length: cover warm‑up; consider trimming transient for metrics.
- Clocking: LF for UI/demo; internal clock for throughput experiments.
- Output: timestamped CSV summary + JSON detail + plots; never overwrite.

### Performance & Scaling Targets
- Throughput: aim near-linear up to target agent counts.
- Overhead: DSM overhead should remain bounded vs centralized queueing growth.
- Freshness: AoI violations below threshold for effective stigmergy.

### Code Organization Rules
- world/: model, agents, graph; no UI/I/O.
- dsm/: memory API + strategies; no agent policy.
- pathfinder/: routing and costs; pure functions using API reads.
- experiments/: orchestration, metrics, plots; no agent control logic.
- dashboard/: UI only; read-only via APIs.
- Extensibility: Add DSM layers/merges without touching agents; inject via DSM interface.

### Testing & Reproducibility
- Smoke tests: short runs for each mode with non-zero completions.
- Determinism: same config+seed → identical summary within tolerance.
- Regression: fixed example configs; compare key metrics to baselines.

### Telemetry & Logging
- Progress logs every ~100 steps: created/completed/active counts, AoI stats.
- Verbose mode opt-in; keep default logging lightweight.

### Dashboard/UI Rules
- Non‑blocking: UI must not gate simulation steps; poll/async reads.
- Visualize: agent states, task locations, congestion heatmaps from DSM layers.

### Glossary
- AoI: Age of Information (ms).
- Halo: Border region shared across partitions.
- Partition Quality: Heuristic for cut size/communication vs balance.